vaccine hesitancy continues to be a  persistent problem in the united states  only 58.8 percent of people 12 and older  in the us are fully vaccinated for some  that's led to difficult conversations  with vaccine hesitant friends and family  so psychiatrist dr sue varma joins us  now with some ideas for navigating these  difficult conversations uh so good to  see you this morning and i'm so happy  we're having a conversation about this  what would be  your advice for people to understand the  best way to approach having a  conversation with a friend a family or a  loved one  vlad great to see you and great to join  you on this topic it's so important that  we have  a sort of open mind neutral stance and  sort of compassionate and empathic  outlook and that might seem like the  farthest thing from our mind right now  because we're saying we are angry we  want to move forward with our lives this  is not safe and there are three things  that i think of we call them the three  c's of vaccine hesitancy is the person  that you're talking to feeling  complacent they might say to themselves  i don't have a risk of getting this  virus and i don't have a risk of getting  sick from it and it's very interesting  because if we look at the unvaccinated  folks 29 of them actually don't think  that they're at risk for getting covet  as compared to 17 percent of vaccinated  people that means they estimate the risk  to them as being much lower the  unvaccinated group the next thing is  confidence do they feel safe do they  feel as if that they're going to benefit  from them and lastly we want to look at  convenience do they think that they're  going to get sick do they think that  they're going to make miss a day of work  are they going to have side effects and  this is so important because when we  look at who the vaccinated unvaccinated  population is a lot of them happen to  also be uninsured and for a lot of  people who are uninsured we're talking  about people who might be  you know don't have facility don't have  access to health care don't have trust  in the health care system so that's  really important for us to understand  the context in which the person is  refusing a vaccine and here's another  thing vlad low vaccine hesitancy doesn't  necessarily mean acceptance so we want  to push them in that direction with  credible information  dr varma the three c's of vaccine  hesitancy there i think a lot of  americans watching might add a fourth  seed ends in y i think you can think  about what it might be and and on that  subject  the reason some of the people we're  dealing with in our personal lives uh  are so confounding to us is because they  bought into theories or ideas that are  completely false online how do you go  about having a conversation with  somebody where you correct them or lead  them toward the best available science  and information  such a great question tony and congrats  on the new board um you know look this  is a very tough situation because a lot  of times  we are butting heads with people when we  keep throwing facts and science which is  great and there are some people who can  work with you on that and some people  who are in that sort of i don't know  give me the information let me feel safe  go with me to the vaccine get me a good  health care provider that's gonna you  know give me the vaccine those people  might be more amenable but if you have  people who are firmly rooted and have  been for years and that's going to tell  you that look you're probably not going  to get a far with them talk about  personal stories talk about people you  know talk about yourself talk about your  own reasons tell them you love them but  what you want to do  is not alienate them and we're going to  end up alienating people when we keep  pushing facts and signs which are great  for some people who can handle it but  for a lot of people they're going to get  defensive and they're going to push you  away so stick with the personal stories  about people who've gotten harmed people  who've died and tell them how you it  makes you feel how afraid you are for  their life how you are afraid of the  people that are around them who might  get sick from them and dr varma in some  cases though you can tell as you get  closer you're not going to reach the  same conclusion or change somebody's  mind sort of two-fold how do you end  that conversation and is it okay if you  cut ties with people over this as we've  heard some people doing  absolutely um and this is a very  delicate question because it's not a one  size fits all approach there might be a  loved one in your life who needs your  help for a variety of reasons and  they're unvaccinated so i think it's  really important that you figure out  what those boundaries are for example  you may not want to invite them to an  indoor gathering right but you might be  willing to meet with them outdoors or  talk to them on the phone  keep that lifeline open keep that line  of communication open if your mental  health is being impacted because i know  a lot of people are saying i don't feel  like i know this person anymore i  thought we were eye to eye i know a lot  of people who are health care providers  who are deciding not to get the vaccine  despite having access to all the  information and taking care of very sick  patients with covid so keeping the lines  of communication open because what we  know is isolation and alienation breeds  misinformation so we don't want to make  the person feel further isolated and  have them go down dark rabbit holes of  conspiracy theories when they're by  themselves such a good point you don't  want to expel people from your life but  you do want to keep yourself safe and  your closest family dr sue varma thank  you very much for those reassuring words
 

